 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections: the server keeps one socket session per connected browser and pushes each committed revision to the sessions subscribed to that event. The socket path carries live updates and HTTPS carries recovery, so a browser that was disconnected or has just reconnected catches up by requesting the revisions it has not yet seen.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The socket tier is operated by the project rather than rented from a hosted realtime provider. This is a deliberate acceptance of operational work: the deployment sizes connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, and the browser client implements reconnect and catch-up.
+
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together. The change store keeps replayable revisions; the delivery node owns socket sessions and the publication of committed revisions to them; the browser node owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up.
 
